SUMMARY:Good People by Patmeena Sabit [option 1]
DESCRIPTION:Novel Lovers of Sonoma County\, CA\n\\* [Tommy Orange\, NYT](h
 ttps://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/03/books/review/good-people-patmeena-sabit.
 html?unlocked_article_code=1.NFE.WCg_.9RX0svI7dnp6&smid=url-share): "Gorge
 ous and powerful debut novel... *Good People* borrows freely from the lang
 uage of cinema and television and even social media\, in which interviewee
 s essentially talk directly to the reader\, as if into a camera... The sto
 ry is told through a polyphony of outsiders — neighbors\, business assoc
 iates\, schoolteachers\, reporters. It is a chorus of “good people” wh
 ose observations\, colored by their own smallness and prejudices\, slowly 
 assemble a portrait of a family they never truly knew... Sabit also speaks
  voluminously about the impossible burden and loneliness of the second-gen
 eration experience. There is a profound sadness in the way the 'good peopl
 e' here settle on a version of truth that satisfies their own worldview.\n
 \n\\* Ed: "18 year old Afghan-American Zorah is dead. Was it an accident o
 r an honor killing? A Greek chorus of other Afghans in a posh Virginia sub
 urb\, neighbors\, journalists\, an anti-violence against women NGO\, a def
 ense attorney all speak -- but we do not hear directly from the principals
  nor is there an omniscient narrator in this finely crafted polyphonic 400
  pager that reads very fast because of the short statements from the choru
 s members. It was a vibrant read. I strongly recommend it."\n\n\\* Lucinda
 : "This book is a stunner. I could not put it down and if you don’t beli
 eve me\, Ann Patchett says it’s a 'thrilling tour de force of a novel.' 
 I was so impressed with Sabit’s ability to tell the story through so man
 y voices but never lose the thread of the narrative. In her Author’s Not
 e she says she wanted to tell a story of not the fact\, but the possibilit
 y of an 'honor crime'” and to tell it at a deeper level with the voices 
 that are largely silent or unsought. It’s a bold book and skillfully wri
 tten. This is her first published novel that took her ten years to complet
 e. I’d highly recommend it."\n\n\\* Debra: "Good People is a stunner. It
 ’s part mystery\, part documentary-style oral history\, a sharp critique
  of the media\, and a deep look at the immigrant experience—all while co
 nstantly forcing you to ask where the line is between actual fact and hear
 say. I recommend not reading too much about the book before you start the 
 book."\n\n\\* Joan: "My ears and mind were glued to the audiobook\, and tw
 o weeks later\, I think about daily. Unusual format: A family is at the ce
 nter of the story\, but it's told by many voices who know or interact or i
 nvestigate the family - never the family itself. I was both emotionally in
 volved and intellectually sparked."\n\nSee reviews\, resources\, and more 
 comments on our Discord.
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/novel-lovers-of-sebastopol/events/317
 432108/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20261114T195730Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261114T195730Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
